<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nafpaktos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Naupaktos, Naupactus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nafpaktos, ancient Naupaktos and Venetian Lepanto, is a port town on the northern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, guarded by a Venetian castle and a walled harbour. It served as an Athenian naval base in the Peloponnesian War, was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1407 and taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1499. The naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> of 1571 took its name from the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nafpaktos.jpg" alt="Nafpaktos" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agnieszka_R · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nafpaktos_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
